Tournament Analysis
  vertices: speed-set time words
  observable: density, cyclic fold, dihedral orbits, odd-sector defect, top cosine mode
  switch: harder = lower density, more fold, fewer quotient orbits, zero odd defect, stronger mode
  fingerprints: {'vertices': ['AP_wall', 'V_star_wall', 'near_AP_apex', 'S562_packet_n14', 'S562_packet_n14_lift', 'no_small_pinch_proxy', 'random_low_resonance', 'all_odd_probe'], 'score_hist': {0: 1, 1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 1, 4: 1, 5: 1, 6: 1, 7: 1}, 'directed_3_cycles': 0, 'sccs': [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1], 'hamiltonian_paths': 1}

Sharper constraints
  1. Cyclic Burnside is still too small: time reversal is always legal.
  2. A counterexample/time-boundary obstruction must live in the dihedral
     fixed sector, hence in the real cosine Fourier subspace.
  3. Nonzero sine/odd character mass is not merely small; it is forbidden.
  4. When cyclic stabilizer is trivial, the binary word still quotients by
     reflection, usually halving lonely slots into t,-t pairs.
  5. The next labelled-event audit should test which owner/core labels remain
     reflection-fixed and which labels break only after forgetting ownership.
